  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nThru Tue...A strong mid level high pressure ridge over the mid MS\nValley this afternoon will shift east across the TN Valley Tue.\nWeak mid level troughs on its southern flank across the northern\nGulf and FL will keep our weather more unsettled. The low level\nridge axis will remain north of the area and continue an onshore\nflow with abundant moisture (PWATs 2.0-2.2\"). This will maintain\nthe general pattern of isolated to scattered showers and \nlightning storms developing over the Atlantic waters in the \novernights and early mornings, some of which push across the \ncoast. Then a higher coverage of additional showers and storms \ndevelop just inland from the coast along and ahead of a \nquick/diffuse east coast sea breeze in the late mornings to early \nafternoons, pushing inland. This will also keep our chances for \nafternoon storms above normal at 50-70% esp over the interior.\nAfternoon and early evening rain chances will be much lower along\nthe coast due to subsidence behind the sea breeze. However, broad \ninstability due to the high moisture and disturbances aloft could \nkick off additional rounds of showers and storms at any point.\n\nWeak steering flow will produce slow moving storms and allow for \nsome erratic cell motions especially along boundary collisions. \nSuch storms could deliver a quick 2-3\" of rainfall leading to \ntemporary flooding of urban, low-lying, or poor drainage areas. \nThere is a very low (less than 5%) chance for one or two spots to \nreceive 4+\" of rainfall, leading to greater flooding impacts. \nStronger storms and heavy showers will be capable of gusty winds \n40-50 mph from water loading. Occasional to frequent cloud to \nground lightning will accompany thunderstorms.\n\nTemperatures remain seasonably hot with highs in the upper 80s to\nlower 90s. But given the high humidity, peak afternoon heat\nindices will remain 100-106. \n\nWed-Thu...The center of the upper ridge reaches the Carolinas as\nthe low level ridge axis begins to inch southward. This is\nforecast to produce a deep southerly flow over our area with even\nhigher PWATS of 2.4-2.5\". Rain chances have increased to 80% over\nthe interior and have collaborated with WPC about adding MRGL\nexcessive rain outlook for Wed (Level 1 of 4). The National \nHurricane Center has reduced its probabilities to near 0% for \nredevelopment of the remnants of \"Dolly\". Nonetheless, its \nmoisture is forecast to reach us Thu, keeping rain chances high \nand a MRGL ERO already in place. \n\nInstability may be more muted in the very saturated atmosphere,\nwarm mid-level temperatures and possibly considerable cloudiness\n(esp Wed), but gusty winds from water loading will remain \npossible. Storms will be capable of occasional to frequent cloud \nto ground lightning. Max temps should be similar in the upper 80s\nand lower 90s.\n\nFri-Sun...Some drier air may temporarily rotate into portions of\nthe area and help to lower rain chances slightly. But at the \nsurface the Bermuda high retreats farther out to to sea, causing \nthe local pressure gradient to become very weak. So will continue\nto show at or above normal rain chances (50-70%). Max temperatures\nwould tick upward into the mid 90s interior if clouds/precip back\noff a bit. Regardless, peak afternoon heat indices remain in the \ntriple digits.",
